---
/**
 * /systems/electrical-platform — the engineering-maturity case study.
 *
 * Same template, same slots, same order as the other two. Framed as what I now
 * do before a system meets its first user, which is why the readiness
 * programme rather than the feature set is the content.
 */
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import ElectricalArchitecture from '../../components/diagrams/ElectricalArchitecture.astro';
import ElectricalTestTiers from '../../components/diagrams/ElectricalTestTiers.astro';

const entry = await getEntry('systems', 'electrical-platform');
if (!entry) throw new Error('Missing content entry: systems/electrical-platform');

const SANITISATION =
  'Staging tenant only. No real organisation name, logo, address, or staff name in frame; no ' +
  'real asset identifiers; browser chrome cropped or a clean profile with no bookmarks, ' +
  'extensions, or console visible.';

const PENDING_GALLERY = [
  'Screenshot 1 of 3 — an electrical inspection checklist mid-submission, mobile 390x844, showing a template item marked faulty.',
  'Screenshot 2 of 3 — the public fault-report flow as a guest sees it after scanning a location code, mobile 390x844.',
  'Screenshot 3 of 3 — the asset registry with an asset in a faulty state, desktop 1440x900, staging data only.',
];

const ownerInputMarkers = PENDING_GALLERY.map(
  (asset) => `<!-- OWNER-INPUT: ${asset} ${SANITISATION} -->`,
).join('\n');
---

<CaseStudyLayout entry={entry}>
  <Fragment slot="context">
    <p>
      This platform runs structured electrical safety inspections on a recurring schedule, lets
      anyone standing in front of a broken thing report it without an account, and tracks the
      physical assets those faults attach to. Five kinds of user, from an unauthenticated guest up
      to a cross-tenant operator.
    </p>
    <p>
      None of which is the reason it is on this site. It has no users. It is pre-launch, and the
      interesting thing about it is what has been done to it while nobody was using it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The hospital platform on this site went into daily production with no automated tests at all.
      That worked, and I would not do it again. This is the system where the lesson was applied
      first and the users came second: a seventeen-package readiness programme, a written
      engineering charter, architecture decisions recorded rather than remembered, and a test suite
      that gates every deploy — all of it before a first customer, not after a first incident.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="constraints">
    <p>
      <strong>No users to learn from.</strong> Everything here had to be reasoned about rather than observed.
      There is no production traffic to profile, no incident history to mine, and no real workload to
      size against — which makes it easy to build for imagined problems and miss real ones.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Still one engineer.</strong> A readiness programme sounds like something a team does. It
      had to be scoped so one person could actually finish it, which is why it is seventeen bounded work
      packages rather than an open-ended quality effort.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Governance has to survive a bad week.</strong> Rules I keep in my head are rules I abandon
      under deadline. If the charter was going to mean anything it had to be written down, enforced in
      review, and inconvenient to ignore.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Tenant isolation is the one thing that cannot be slightly wrong.</strong> Every other defect
      is a bug; that one is a breach. It needed a stronger guarantee than my own carefulness.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="architecture">
    <ElectricalArchitecture />
    <div class="prose body-copy lifecycle">
      <p>
        One process, one database, four capability modules an organisation can switch on
        individually. The boundaries between them are structural rather than networked — a
        deliberate choice recorded as an architecture decision, along with the rule that adding a
        queue, a cache server, or a second service is no longer something a single change can do
        quietly.
      </p>
      <p>
        Authentication is re-verified on every request rather than trusted from the token: the
        account is reloaded, its active flag checked, tokens issued before the last credential reset
        rejected, and the organisation's own status confirmed. Workers carry a longer session than
        staff, because a field device stays signed in for weeks, and are further limited to the
        locations they actually cover — belonging to the organisation is not enough to touch a
        ticket in it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="security">
    <p>
      Every tenant-owned query is scoped by an organisation identifier taken from the verified
      token. Never from a body, never from a query string, never from a URL. A client may state an
      organisation and the server will compare it and reject a mismatch, but it will never act on
      it. Mutations by bare identifier are preceded by an ownership check, so a correctly-guessed
      identifier from another organisation finds nothing.
    </p>
    <p>
      That rule is written down in the charter, was verified once by reading every route file, and
      is now held in place by tests — one regression file per tenant-owned resource area. Each
      asserts that a second organisation receives a not-found rather than a forbidden, because a
      forbidden response confirms the resource exists and that is itself a leak.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is one cross-tenant capability, and it is the most heavily audited thing in the system:
      an operator can act inside a named organisation, and every request made that way is logged —
      reads included, not just writes. Because those entries carry the real organisation identifier,
      they appear in that organisation's own audit log. The people whose data was accessed can see
      that it was.
    </p>
    <p>
      Credential recovery returns the same response and takes the same time whether or not an
      account exists, which closes the timing channel that identical wording alone leaves open.
      Personal fields are encrypted at rest unconditionally — not gated on an environment variable,
      because that is the gate someone forgets.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="operations">
    <p>
      A broken change cannot reach production. Lint and the full test suite run on every push,
      against a real disposable database so the integration tier is never skipped in CI, and the
      deploy job only runs after they pass. The deploy then polls a readiness endpoint that actually
      checks the database, rather than the liveness endpoint that always returns healthy.
    </p>
    <h3>Three tiers, and one that can hide</h3>
    <p>
      The suite is deliberately tiered. Unit tests need nothing. Smoke tests exercise the entire
      HTTP stack in memory, with no listener and no database. Integration tests need a real database
      — and when there isn't one they skip, loudly, with a message saying so.
    </p>
    <ElectricalTestTiers />
    <div class="prose body-copy lifecycle">
      <p>
        That skip is the right behaviour on a laptop and would be a silent hole in CI, so CI always
        provides a database. It is the tier most able to quietly stop running, which is why it is
        the one worth pointing at.
      </p>
      <p>
        Resilience is wired into shared primitives rather than left to each call site: the database
        circuit breaker lives in the query client, the provider breaker inside the one function
        every external call goes through, and scheduled jobs take a database advisory lock so only
        one instance runs a given tick. Nobody has to remember any of it, which is the point — the
        previous arrangement depended on memory and had gaps nobody could see.
      </p>
      <p>
        Backups run daily to offsite storage and the restore path is scripted, with no default
        target and a typed confirmation. The last drill ran against a development-sized dataset, so
        its restore time says nothing useful about production, and the recovery point is up to a
        day. Both are written down rather than assumed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="outcomes">
    <p>
      There are no usage figures here because there is no usage. Everything below describes
      engineering practice, measured at the last readiness audit, and none of it is evidence that
      this system survives contact with real users — because it has not met any.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="owner-input" set:html={ownerInputMarkers} />
</CaseStudyLayout>

<style>
  .lifecycle {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-8);
  }
</style>
